      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular weight:** ≈ 18.015 g/mol.\n- **Structure:** Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) at 1 atm | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, high surface tension, excellent solvent. |\n| **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Invisible gas; condenses back to liquid when cooled. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It Matters |\n|----------|----------------|\n| **Polarity** | Enables water to dissolve many ionic and polar substances → “universal solvent.” |\n| **Hydrogen bonding** | Strong intermolecular forces give water high boiling point, high specific heat, high heat of vaporization, and high surface tension. |\n| **High specific heat (4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹)** | Buffers temperature changes in oceans, lakes, and living organisms. |\n| **Density anomaly** | Maximum density at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing life to survive under frozen surfaces. |\n| **Capillary action** | Water climbs narrow tubes (e.g., plant xylem) due to adhesion and cohesion. |\n| **Transparency** | Allows sunlight to penetrate aquatic ecosystems, supporting photosynthesis. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Life\n- **Hydrologic Cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat. This cycle distributes heat, shapes climate, and replenishes freshwater supplies.\n- **Biological Functions:**  \n  - **Solvent:** Medium for biochemical reactions.  \n  - **Transport:** Carries nutrients, waste, and gases in blood and plant sap.  \n  - **Temperature regulation:** Sweating, respiration, and evaporative cooling.  \n  - **Structural:** Provides turgor pressure in plant cells; forms the basis of cellular compartments.  \n- **Geochemical Processes:** Weathering of rocks, formation of minerals, and transport of dissolved ions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sources & Availability\n- **Freshwater:** ~2.5 % of Earth’s water; most is locked in glaciers, ice caps, and groundwater. Only ~0.3 % is readily accessible surface water (rivers, lakes).  \n- **Saltwater:** ~97.5 % of global water, primarily in the oceans (average salinity ≈ 35 ‰).  \n- **Atmospheric water:** Small but crucial component (water vapor, clouds, precipitation).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Essential for human health; typically treated to remove pathogens and contaminants.  \n- **Cooking & cleaning:** Solvent properties dissolve sugars, salts, fats, and soils.  \n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, steam generation, hydroelectric power, etc.  \n- **Recreation:** Swimming, sailing, hydrotherapy.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Takeaway\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonded molecule (H₂O) that exists naturally as ice, liquid, and vapor. Its unique physical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, solvent ability, and density anomaly—make it indispensable for Earth’s climate, geological processes, and all known forms of life."
